<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boeotia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Boiotia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boeotia is a region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, whose fertile plains were dominated in antiquity by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Its cities, including Orchomenus, Thespiae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, formed the Boeotian League, and it was the scene of major battles such as Plataea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> and Chaeronea. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a>, Thebes briefly became the leading Greek power in the 360s BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boeotia.jpg" alt="Boeotia" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Badseed; derivative work by Pitichinaccio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2010_Dimi_Viotias_numbered.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Boeotia</b>, the fertile plain of central Greece between Attica and Phocis, held a dozen or more independent cities, of which Thebes was the largest, followed by Orchomenus, Thespiae, Tanagra and Plataea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Above the city stood the Amphictyonic League, or Amphictyony, an ancient association of twelve peoples, mostly of central and northern Greece, among them the Thessalians, <b>Boeotians</b>, Phocians, Locrians, Dorians and Ionians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Mycenae, Tiryns, the Acropolis of Athens and the vast fortress of Gla in <b>Boeotia</b> were walled in this way, and in the 13th century BCE Mycenae, Tiryns and Athens built protected passages down to water sources, as if expecting sieges.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Thebans and their Boeotian allies under Epaminondas defeated a Spartan army near Leuctra in <b>Boeotia</b>, killing King Cleombrotus I. The defeat ended the belief in Spartan invincibility and began a decade of Theban supremacy in Greece.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Twelve peoples, among them the Thessalians, Phocians, <b>Boeotians</b>, Dorians and Ionians, each sent two delegates (hieromnemones) to a council meeting twice a year at Anthela and Delphi.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>Eastern mainland Greece, including Attica, <b>Boeotia</b>, Phocis and Locris, joined the revolution in 1821.</blockquote><a href="/state/areopagus-of-eastern-continental-greece">Areopagus of Eastern Continental Greece</a></li></ul></section>
